Here’s a simple list of common Linux commands used in DevOps with who uses them, what they do, and why:

✅ 1. ls
	•	Who: Everyone
	•	What it does: Lists files and folders in a directory
	•	Why: To check what’s inside a directory
	•	Example: ls -l (shows details like permissions and size)

✅ 2. cd
	•	Who: Everyone
	•	What it does: Changes directory
	•	Why: To move into a specific folder
	•	Example: cd /var/log

✅ 3. pwd
	•	Who: Everyone
	•	What it does: Shows current directory
	•	Why: To confirm where you are in the file system
	•	Example: pwd

✅ 4. cat
	•	Who: Developers, SREs
	•	What it does: Displays file content
	•	Why: To quickly read log or config files
	•	Example: cat config.yaml

✅ 5. tail
	•	Who: DevOps, Support
	•	What it does: Shows the last lines of a file
	•	Why: To view the latest logs
	•	Example: tail -f /var/log/syslog

✅ 6. grep
	•	Who: Everyone
	•	What it does: Searches text in files
	•	Why: To find specific info (like errors) in logs
	•	Example: grep "error" /var/log/syslog

✅ 7. chmod
	•	Who: DevOps, Admins
	•	What it does: Changes file permissions
	•	Why: To allow or deny access to users
	•	Example: chmod +x script.sh (makes script executable)

✅ 8. chown
	•	Who: DevOps, Admins
	•	What it does: Changes file owner
	•	Why: To give file access to another user
	•	Example: chown user1 file.txt

✅ 9. ps
	•	Who: DevOps, Admins
	•	What it does: Shows running processes
	•	Why: To check if a service/app is running
	•	Example: ps aux | grep nginx

✅ 10. kill
	•	Who: Admins
	•	What it does: Stops a running process
	•	Why: To stop a frozen or unneeded process
	•	Example: kill 1234 (where 1234 is the process ID)

✅ 11. top or htop
	•	Who: DevOps
	•	What it does: Shows real-time system usage
	•	Why: To monitor CPU, memory, and process activity
	•	Example: top

✅ 12. df -h
	•	Who: DevOps
	•	What it does: Shows disk space usage
	•	Why: To check if disk is full
	•	Example: df -h

✅ 13. du -sh
	•	Who: DevOps
	•	What it does: Shows folder size
	•	Why: To see how much space a folder is using
	•	Example: du -sh /var/log

✅ 14. scp
	•	Who: DevOps, Engineers
	•	What it does: Securely copies files between servers
	•	Why: To move configs/scripts
	•	Example: scp file.txt user@server:/home/user/

✅ 15. ssh
	•	Who: Everyone
	•	What it does: Connects to a remote Linux server
	•	Why: To manage servers remotely
	•	Example: ssh user@server

✅ 16. crontab -e
	•	Who: DevOps
	•	What it does: Edits scheduled jobs (cron jobs)
	•	Why: To automate tasks
	•	Example: Run a script every day at 2 AM
